> file 2> /dev/null -
> file = save successful things in file (overwrites if the file exist)
2> /dev/null = any error messagges will be throw here
> file 1> /dev/null -
> file = save successfully things in file
1> /dev/null = this command overwrite the first command. in linux, the last command will imply. so, the success command will throw in /dev/null.
 screen show error output
&> file - both success and error message are saved in file
>> file 2>&1 - notice the 2 on the left. This means "send errors to where stream 1 is currently going".
  
  
